* START-HISTORY:
* 20 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - written for SOLO 74 (sit8, 20 Nov 2026; the owner's "option 1").  Each job had a Task Scheduler task of its own,
*           registered by the doors helper as SYSTEM; the VM measured that SYSTEM may not register another account's S4U task ("Access is
*           denied", as row 280 found on 13 Nov), so no job could be made.  Now ONE task, "\SD Core Solo Jobs\sd-solo-jobs-clock", is
*           registered by the installer's elevated step AS THE USER (which Windows allows) and runs sd-solo.exe run.jobs.due every minute,
*           signed in or out; this program decides what is due from the definitions in $jobs.  Windows Solo only: Linux Solo's jobs are the
*           user's own systemd timers, which need no elevation.  A deviation forced by the operating system, listed for the parity audit.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   RUN.JOBS.DUE        the whole of the job clock's command line: <Solo folder>\usr\bin\sd-solo.exe run.jobs.due
*
*   ONLY AS THAT ONE-SHOT: the session's single command (system(1026)) must be exactly "run.jobs.due".  Login admits it as it admits run.job
*   (the kept password while the user is signed in; the Windows identity route while signed out, gpl.bp/login RUN-JOB-IDENTITY).
*
*   WITH JOBS OFF (SET.JOBS.ACCESS) it does nothing and says nothing: it runs every minute, and RUN.JOB refuses and audits a job anyway.
*
*   A JOB IS DUE when the latest time its schedule names (!SOLO_JOB_TIMES) is later than its last run (field 6 of its definition) and than
*   the moment it was set (fields 4 and 5), and is not more than five minutes ago - a run missed while the computer was off, or while SD
*   could not start, is not made up.  SET.JOB RUN marks a job to run at the next tick (field 7, "now").  Every due job is stamped first,
*   under its record lock, so a second tick that overlaps this one does not run it again; then each is run, one after another, by
*   EXECUTEing "run.job <name>", which checks the switch and the definition again and audits the start and the end.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program run.jobs.due
$catalog $runjobsdue

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

$define LATE.LIMIT 300

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
   acc.name = 'sduser'

   one = downcase(trim(system(1026)))
   if one # 'run.jobs.due' or dcount(trim(@sentence), ' ') # 1 then
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'run.jobs.due refused reason=not the job clock one-shot')
      stop sysmsg(12930)                       ;* run.jobs.due runs only as the job clock's own command
   end

   sw.on = @false
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'accounts' to sw.f then
      read sw.rec from sw.f, acc.name then
         if upcase(trim(sw.rec<ACC$JOBS>)) = 'YES' then sw.on = @true
      end
      close sw.f
   end
   @system.return.code = 0
   if not(sw.on) then stop

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : '$jobs' to jobs.f else stop
   now = date() * 86400 + int(time())

*  First pass: decide and stamp.  A record another tick holds is left to that tick.
   due = ''
   select jobs.f to 11
   loop
      readnext jname from 11 else exit
      gosub name.ok
      if not(ok) then continue
      readu rec from jobs.f, jname locked
         continue
      end then
         is.due = @false
         if downcase(trim(rec<7>)) = 'now' then
            is.due = @true
         end else
            call !SOLO_JOB_TIMES(rec<1>, now, prev, next)
            if prev # '' then
               since = (if rec<4> matches '1N0N' and rec<5> matches '1N0N' then rec<4> * 86400 + rec<5> else 0)
               if rec<6> matches '1N0N' then
                  if rec<6> > since then since = rec<6>
               end
               if prev > since and prev > now - LATE.LIMIT then is.due = @true
            end
         end
         if is.due then
            rec<6> = now
            rec<7> = ''
            write rec to jobs.f, jname on error
               release jobs.f, jname
               continue
            end
            due<-1> = jname
         end else
            release jobs.f, jname
         end
      end else
         release jobs.f, jname
      end
   repeat
   close jobs.f

*  Second pass: run them.  RUN.JOB checks everything again.
   n = dcount(due, @fm)
   for i = 1 to n
      execute 'run.job ' : due<i>
   next i
   @system.return.code = 0
   stop

* jname in; ok out.  SET.JOB's rule.
name.ok:
   ok = @false
   if len(jname) < 1 or len(jname) > 32 then return
   if index('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz', jname[1, 1], 1) = 0 then return
   if jname[len(jname), 1] = '-' then return
   if convert('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-', '', jname) # '' then return
   ok = @true
   return

end

* END-CODE
